92186417632 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417676 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417647 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417676 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417676 1077 "selection" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417676 1078 #edb/ref 17592186417618 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417676 1079 #uuid "f6ce8e2c-2c01-51f1-9dd8-1153184ac72a" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417669 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417677 1069 "q-340755" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417677 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417677 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic extensions of some functions from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $4\\text{th}$ Fourier cosine coefficient of $4f(x)-g(x)?$" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417677 1072 #uuid "15446e45-99ce-5477-87bc-257fadf83d70" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417677 1074 "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x)+\\beta g(x)∼\\frac{\\alpha {a}_{0}+\\beta {c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\alpha {a}_{n}+\\beta {c}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+(\\alpha {b}_{n}+\\beta {d}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $4f(x)-g(x)$ are\n\n- $4{a}_{0}-{c}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $4{a}_{n}-{c}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $4{b}_{n}-{d}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $4$th cosine coefficient of $4f(x)-g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n4{a}_{4}-{c}_{4}.\n$$" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417678 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417676 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417678 1069 "q-340767" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417678 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417678 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic extensions of some functions from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $5\\text{th}$ Fourier sine coefficient of $4f(x)-g(x)?$" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417678 1072 #uuid "236925ac-8f18-52a6-be78-880a69837dd3" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417678 1074 "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x)+\\beta g(x)∼\\frac{\\alpha {a}_{0}+\\beta {c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\alpha {a}_{n}+\\beta {c}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+(\\alpha {b}_{n}+\\beta {d}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $4f(x)-g(x)$ are\n\n- $4{a}_{0}-{c}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $4{a}_{n}-{c}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $4{b}_{n}-{d}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $5$th sine coefficient of $4f(x)-g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n4{b}_{5}-{d}_{5}.\n$$" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417665 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417679 1069 "q-340777" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417679 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417679 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above.  What is the constant Fourier coefficient of  $f(x)-5?$" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417679 1072 #uuid "246856ef-7968-5c5f-8c50-2abd9acd3996" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417679 1074 "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that if $C$ is a constant, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(x)+C$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}+2C$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- ${b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nIn our case, $C=-5.$ Therefore, the constant Fourier coefficient of  $f(x)-5$ is ${a}_{0}-10.$" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417671 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417680 1069 "q-340953" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417680 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $5$th sine Fourier coefficient of $f(x)+5?$" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417680 1072 #uuid "2dba35b3-124d-5cf7-913a-b8717960d9c4" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417680 1074 "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that if $C$ is a constant, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(x)+C$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}+2C$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- ${b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nIn our case, $C=5.$ Therefore, the $5$th sine coefficient of $f(x)+5$ is ${b}_{5}$ (remains unchanged)." 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417668 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417681 1069 "q-340775" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417681 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417681 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $3$rd cosine Fourier coefficient of $f(x)-4?$" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417681 1072 #uuid "562791e1-e442-5168-b91d-ede177859b61" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417681 1074 "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that if $C$ is a constant, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(x)+C$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}+2C$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- ${b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nIn our case, $C=-4.$ Therefore, the $3$rd cosine coefficient of $f(x)-4$ is ${a}_{3}$ (remains unchanged)." 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417664 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417682 1069 "q-340761" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417682 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417682 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic extensions of some functions from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the constant Fourier coefficient of  $3f(x)+6g(x)?$" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417682 1072 #uuid "86928bbe-ce4d-5454-a36f-1b092e7b6f03" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417682 1074 "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x)+\\beta g(x)∼\\frac{\\alpha {a}_{0}+\\beta {c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\alpha {a}_{n}+\\beta {c}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+(\\alpha {b}_{n}+\\beta {d}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $3f(x)+6g(x)$ are\n\n- $3{a}_{0}+6{c}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $3{a}_{n}+6{c}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $3{b}_{n}+6{d}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the constant Fourier coefficient of $3f(x)+6g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n3{a}_{0}+6{c}_{0}.\n$$" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417672 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417674 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417683 1069 "q-340804" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417683 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417683 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417683 1072 #uuid "c24953c9-584d-5ff1-a2f7-1addba6f623e" 13194139534287 17592186045417 true] [17592186417683 1074 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139534287 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-07a132ee-c703-5c0b-949d-512c03d51f8b" 17592186417617 "answer-095dc